00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Thanks for having me, Shankar. You can't tell the story of solitary confinement without understanding the story of a man named George Jackson. He was sent to prison in the early 1960s after pleading guilty to armed robbery, and he was sentenced to a term of one year to life. What was the thinking behind this kind of indeterminate sentence? So the idea for someone like George Jackson was that he would go to prison and he'd have to prove that he'd been rehabilitated before he could get out of prison. Around the time Jackson went to prison, however, people started to look at these indeterminate sentences
谢谢邀请我，香卡。如果不了解乔治·杰克逊这个人的故事，你就无法讲述单人禁闭的故事。他在20世纪60年代初因承认武装抢劫而被送进监狱，并被判处一年至无期徒刑。这种不定期刑期背后的想法是什么？所以，对乔治·杰克逊这样的人的想法是，他进入监狱后，必须证明自己已经改过自新，然后才能离开监狱。然而，在大约杰克逊入狱的时期，人们开始审视这些不定期刑期——

6
00:02:47.000 --> 00:03:12.000
and realize that they were having truly disproportionate impacts depending on the race of the person with the sentence. So white people had a much easier time convincing prison officials that they had been reformed and should be let out at prison than African-American men like George Jackson happened to be. So George Jackson found himself essentially stuck in prison, years went by when he was denied parole, and he became radicalized in this process.
并意识到它们产生了真正不成比例的影响，这取决于受刑者的种族。因此，白人要让监狱官员相信他们已经改过自新并应该被释放，比乔治·杰克逊这样的非裔美国人要容易得多。因此，乔治·杰克逊发现自己基本上被困在监狱里，多年过去了，他的假释申请一直被拒绝，在这个过程中，他变得激进化了。

7
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:41.000
He wrote a book of best-selling letters that achieved national and international acclaim, letters to his family and to his lawyers, articulating his revolutionary politics and the problems with things like this indeterminate sentence. This brought him to the attention of prison officials, and he was accused of murdering a prison guard in the early 1970s, and he was preemptively sent to death row at San Quentin while he awaited a death penalty trial for that murder he was accused of.
他写了一本畅销书信集，在国内外获得了高度赞誉——写给家人和律师的信，表达了他的革命政治观点以及像这种不定期刑期之类的问题所带来的弊端。这引起了监狱官员的注意，在20世纪70年代初，他被指控谋杀了一名狱警，在等待该谋杀指控的死刑审判期间，他被先发制人地送到了圣昆廷监狱的死囚牢房。

8
00:03:41.000 --> 00:04:19.000
And while on death row at San Quentin in an isolation unit, interestingly, one day his lawyer came in to visit him. And the story that prison officials tell and that's been repeated many times is that his lawyer snuck a nine millimeter gun into him inside of a tape recorder. And that Jackson then used that gun to try to escape from this isolation unit. All we actually know is that he was shot to death on the San Quentin prison yard as he ran out of the isolation unit. And when staff ran into the unit to see what had happened, they found three officers and two more prisoners who'd been stabbed to death.
而在圣昆廷监狱隔离区的死囚牢房里，有趣的是，有一天他的律师来看望他。监狱官员讲述并被多次重复的故事是，他的律师把一把九毫米手枪藏在录音机里带给了他。然后杰克逊用那把枪试图逃离这个隔离区。我们实际知道的全部是，当他跑出隔离区时，他在圣昆廷监狱的院子里被枪杀。当工作人员冲进牢房查看发生了什么时，他们发现三名警官和另外两名囚犯被刺身亡。

9
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:41.000
So this was the most violent day in California's prison history ever. Six deaths total on August 21st, 1971. And this moment is a moment that people point to, in California, as incredibly important in understanding why the state needed long-term solitary confinement units. And similar things happened across the US, and prison officials in other states point to those moments.
所以这是加州监狱史上最暴力的一天。1971年8月21日，总共有六人死亡。人们指出，在加州，这一时刻对于理解为什么该州需要长期单人禁闭室具有极其重要的意义。美国各地也发生了类似的事情，其他州的监狱官员也指出过这些时刻。

10
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:59.000
